-- AWS Security Specialty (SCS-C02) Domain 6 expansion: adds flashcards sc-f-700..730, sc-a-200..206, sc-y-100..103 and questions sc-q-700..733 covering 6.1 centralized account management (Organizations policy types, SCPs, RCPs, declarative policies, Control Tower), 6.2 multi-account strategy (landing-zone account roles, OU design, blast-radius isolation), and 6.3 evaluating compliance (Config aggregators/conformance packs, Security Hub standards, Trusted Advisor, Audit Manager, AWS Artifact).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 6.1 centralized AWS account management: core
('sc-f-700','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What must an AWS Organization be enabled with before Service Control Policies or tag policies take effect?$$,$$All features mode - the full Organizations capability set (SCPs, tag policies, and other policy types). Consolidated billing only, the older/legacy mode, supports centralized billing but not policy enforcement.$$),
('sc-f-701','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$How do SCPs at different levels of an OU hierarchy combine for a single account?$$,$$Effective permissions are the intersection of the SCPs attached at every level from the organization root down through each parent OU to the account itself - an explicit deny at any level blocks the action everywhere beneath it, and nothing is allowed unless every level in the chain permits it.$$),
('sc-f-702','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$Besides Service Control Policies, what other policy types does AWS Organizations support?$$,$$Tag policies (standardize tags), backup policies (enforce AWS Backup plans), resource control policies (cap what resource-based policies can grant), AI services opt-out policies, and declarative policies (enforce specific account settings without drift) - each governs a different aspect of member accounts alongside SCPs.$$),
('sc-f-703','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the difference between an SCP "deny list" strategy and an "allow list" strategy?$$,$$Deny list (the default) keeps the built-in FullAWSAccess allow and layers explicit Deny statements to block specific actions. Allow list replaces FullAWSAccess with an explicit Allow naming only permitted services/actions, so anything not listed is implicitly denied - stricter but requires careful maintenance as needs grow.$$),
('sc-f-704','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the difference between a preventive and a detective Control Tower guardrail?$$,$$A preventive guardrail is implemented as an SCP that blocks a disallowed action before it can happen. A detective guardrail is implemented as an AWS Config rule that flags a resource already out of compliance, for alerting or remediation after the fact.$$),
('sc-f-705','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What are the three tiers of Control Tower guardrails?$$,$$Mandatory (always enforced, cannot be disabled - e.g., don't disable CloudTrail), strongly recommended (best-practice guardrails you can enable but aren't forced into), and elective (optional guardrails for specific use cases you opt into).$$),
('sc-f-706','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What does AWS Control Tower Account Factory do?$$,$$Automates provisioning of new member accounts with a pre-approved, standardized baseline (network config, guardrails, IAM baseline) already applied, removing manual per-account setup and configuration drift at account creation time.$$),
('sc-f-707','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What does AWS Control Tower drift detection identify?$$,$$Cases where a Control Tower-governed account's or OU's resources have been changed outside of Control Tower (e.g., an SCP or IAM role manually edited), diverging from the landing zone's intended baseline - flagged so the drift can be reviewed and reverted.$$),
('sc-f-708','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$How does a Resource Control Policy (RCP) differ from a Service Control Policy (SCP)?$$,$$An SCP caps the maximum permissions of identities (IAM users/roles) calling AWS. An RCP caps the maximum permissions that can be granted through resource-based policies (e.g., an S3 bucket policy) attached to resources in member accounts - it protects the resource side rather than the identity side.$$),
('sc-f-709','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What problem do AWS Organizations declarative policies solve?$$,$$They let you enforce a specific account-level setting (e.g., block public access for new EBS snapshots or AMIs) org-wide as a declared desired state, so the setting can't be changed or drift back to a less secure value in any member account.$$),
-- 6.1 centralized AWS account management: acronym
('sc-a-200','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.1',$$RCP$$,$$Resource Control Policy - an AWS Organizations policy type that sets the maximum permissions grantable through resource-based policies across member accounts.$$),
('sc-a-201','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.1',$$AFT$$,$$Account Factory for Terraform - a Control Tower-integrated solution that provisions and customizes new accounts using Terraform instead of the built-in Account Factory console workflow.$$),
-- 6.1 centralized AWS account management: feynman
('sc-y-100','awsscs','feynman',6,'6.1',$$Explain how Service Control Policies, Resource Control Policies, and permission boundaries each cap access differently, and why a company might need all three.$$,$$Model answer: Think of it as three independent ceilings stacked on different things. An SCP caps what any identity (user or role) in an account or OU can ever do, no matter what its own IAM policy grants - it's enforced by Organizations, outside the account's own IAM control, and even a full admin can't override it. A permission boundary caps a single specific IAM user or role's maximum permissions, set inside the account, mainly used to safely let developers self-service create roles without fear they grant themselves too much. A Resource Control Policy caps the other direction: not what an identity can do, but what a resource-based policy (like an S3 bucket policy) is allowed to grant to anyone, including principals outside the account. A company running a multi-account, multi-tenant setup might need an SCP to enforce "no account can ever touch billing," a permission boundary so a delegated admin's self-created roles can't become AdministratorAccess, and an RCP so no bucket in any account can be accidentally opened to the public internet or an untrusted account - three different attack surfaces, three different caps.$$),
-- 6.2 multi-account security strategy: core
('sc-f-710','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the purpose of a dedicated Log Archive account in an AWS landing zone?$$,$$It receives and stores CloudTrail, Config, and other logs from every account in the organization, with restrictive access (often read-only for the security team) so that even a fully compromised workload or management account cannot reach or delete the evidence held there.$$),
('sc-f-711','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What role does a dedicated Security (Audit) Tooling account play in a landing zone?$$,$$It hosts the delegated-administrator configuration for services like GuardDuty, Security Hub, and Config aggregators, and provides the security team cross-account, typically read-only, roles into every other account - centralizing detection and investigation tooling away from workload risk.$$),
('sc-f-712','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$Why do landing zones often place shared networking (like a Transit Gateway or centralized egress) in its own dedicated account?$$,$$Centralizing shared connectivity in one network account means workload accounts don't need their own internet-facing infrastructure or direct peering to each other, shrinking each workload account's own attack surface and letting the network team apply consistent controls in one place.$$),
('sc-f-713','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is a common top-level OU design beneath the organization root in a landing zone?$$,$$Separate OUs for Security (log archive, audit/tooling accounts), Infrastructure/Shared Services (network, shared tooling), and Workloads (further split into Sandbox, Dev, Test, and Prod OUs) - each OU boundary lets different SCPs and guardrails apply to fundamentally different risk levels.$$),
('sc-f-714','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is a Suspended OU used for in a landing zone?$$,$$A holding area for accounts being decommissioned or under investigation - a highly restrictive SCP (often denying nearly everything) is applied so the account's data and evidence are preserved but nothing can be actively used, before eventual closure.$$),
('sc-f-715','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$Why might a newly vended AWS account start in a Quarantine OU before moving to its intended workload OU?$$,$$A restrictive SCP in the Quarantine OU limits what the brand-new account can do until required baseline configuration (logging, guardrails, tagging) is confirmed in place, preventing a misconfigured or not-yet-validated account from creating risk from day one.$$),
('sc-f-716','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$Why do landing zones typically separate production and non-production workloads into different AWS accounts rather than different VPCs in one account?$$,$$Account boundaries are a much stronger isolation line than VPC boundaries - IAM, SCPs, service quotas, and blast radius are all scoped per account, so a mistake or compromise in a dev/test account has no direct path to production resources, credentials, or data.$$),
('sc-f-717','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is a break-glass account/procedure in a multi-account strategy?$$,$$A tightly controlled emergency-access path (often a dedicated IAM identity with strong MFA and heavy alerting) reserved for when normal federated access is unavailable, used rarely and audited heavily, so responders can still act during an identity-provider outage without leaving standing broad access open day to day.$$),
('sc-f-718','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the security purpose of a Sandbox OU/account in a landing zone?$$,$$It gives developers a space to experiment freely with no connectivity to production data or shared resources, so mistakes or insecure configurations made while learning or prototyping can't affect anything that matters.$$),
('sc-f-719','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$Why might a company create separate AWS accounts per region or jurisdiction rather than one global account?$$,$$Regulatory or data-residency requirements sometimes mandate that certain data and processing stay within a specific country/region; isolating that workload in its own account (or OU) makes it far easier to prove and enforce the boundary than relying on discipline within a single shared account.$$),
('sc-f-720','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the benefit of giving each business unit its own OU in AWS Organizations?$$,$$Each business unit can have SCPs, tag policies, and cost allocation tailored to its own risk profile and budget, and a security incident or misconfiguration in one BU's accounts stays contained within that OU's boundary rather than affecting others.$$),
('sc-f-721','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$Why does vending every new account through a standardized landing-zone process (rather than creating accounts ad hoc) reduce blast radius?$$,$$Every account starts with the same guardrails, logging, and baseline configuration already in place, so there's no window where a freshly created account is unmonitored or missing required controls - inconsistency at creation time is a common source of accounts that later become the weak link.$$),
('sc-f-722','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the purpose of testing a new SCP in a Policy Staging OU before applying it organization-wide?$$,$$It limits the blast radius of a mistake in the policy itself - a small, non-critical set of accounts absorbs any unintended breakage first, so a typo or overly broad Deny doesn't simultaneously lock every production account out of a needed action.$$),
-- 6.2 multi-account security strategy: acronym
('sc-a-202','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.2',$$LZA$$,$$Landing Zone Accelerator on AWS - an AWS-provided solution that automates deploying a multi-account landing zone (OUs, SCPs, logging, networking) aligned to security best practices, faster than building the structure by hand.$$),
-- 6.2 multi-account security strategy: feynman
('sc-y-101','awsscs','feynman',6,'6.2',$$Explain why a company would split its AWS footprint into a dozen accounts instead of just using IAM permissions inside one account to separate teams and environments.$$,$$Model answer: IAM only controls who can do what inside a boundary that's still shared - service quotas, blast radius, and even a single compromised credential's reach are all scoped to the account, not to an IAM policy. If dev, test, and prod all live in one account, a bug in a dev deployment script with slightly too-broad permissions, or one leaked credential, can potentially touch production data, because nothing stops it at the account edge. Splitting into separate accounts means a mistake or compromise in one account simply cannot reach resources in another without an explicit, auditable cross-account trust relationship you set up on purpose. It also lets you apply completely different guardrails per account - a sandbox account can allow almost anything because nothing there matters, while a production account can be locked down hard - something a single shared IAM policy set struggles to express cleanly at scale.$$),
('sc-y-102','awsscs','feynman',6,'6.2',$$Explain the difference in purpose between a Log Archive account and a Security Tooling/Audit account in a landing zone, and why they're usually not the same account.$$,$$Model answer: The Log Archive account's only job is to be a vault - it receives logs from everywhere and holds them immutably, with access locked down so tightly that almost nobody, including most of the security team, can write or delete there day to day. The Security Tooling account is where the actual work happens - GuardDuty, Security Hub, and Config aggregators run there, analysts log in there, and it needs broader (if still tightly scoped) read access into every other account to do its job. If you merged them, the account doing active investigative work - the one analysts touch daily and that has read access everywhere - would also be the one holding the tamper-evident evidence, so a mistake or compromise on the tooling side could put the evidence itself at risk. Keeping them separate means the vault stays untouched even if something goes wrong on the working side.$$),
-- 6.3 evaluate compliance of AWS resources: core
('sc-f-723','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$What does an AWS Config aggregator provide?$$,$$A single, consolidated view of AWS Config data (resource configuration and rule compliance) across multiple accounts and regions, so the security team can see compliance status org-wide without logging into every account individually.$$),
('sc-f-724','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$Name the primary security standards AWS Security Hub can check an account against.$$,$$The AWS Foundational Security Best Practices (FSBP) standard, the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, the NIST 800-53 standard, and PCI DSS - each is a curated set of automated checks Security Hub can enable and score compliance against.$$),
('sc-f-725','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$What is the Security Hub security score?$$,$$A percentage reflecting how many enabled controls across your active standards currently pass versus fail, giving a single, trackable number for overall security posture that teams can watch improve (or regress) over time.$$),
('sc-f-726','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$What does Security Hub central configuration let you do?$$,$$Enable and manage Security Hub standards and controls across every account and region in an organization from one designated administrator account, instead of configuring Security Hub separately in each account/region.$$),
('sc-f-727','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$How can AWS Config automatically fix a resource it finds noncompliant, rather than only reporting it?$$,$$Attach a remediation action, typically an SSM Automation document, to the Config rule; when a resource is evaluated as noncompliant, Config can trigger that automation to remediate it automatically or after manual approval.$$),
('sc-f-728','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$What does the Trusted Advisor organizational view provide?$$,$$Lets the management (or a delegated) account view Trusted Advisor check results aggregated across every member account from one place, instead of a security team having to log into each account separately to see its checks.$$),
('sc-f-729','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$What do AWS Audit Manager's prebuilt frameworks (CIS, PCI DSS, GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2, and others) provide?$$,$$Pre-mapped control-to-evidence-source relationships for well-known compliance frameworks, so evidence collection starts working immediately against a recognized standard instead of the customer having to define every control-to-evidence mapping themselves.$$),
('sc-f-730','awsscs','core',6,'6.3',$$How does AWS Artifact differ from AWS Audit Manager?$$,$$Artifact is a self-service portal for downloading AWS's own compliance reports and agreements (SOC reports, PCI attestation, a BAA) - it tells you about AWS's compliance. Audit Manager collects evidence about your own workloads running on AWS, mapped to a chosen framework - it tells you about your compliance.$$),
-- 6.3 evaluate compliance of AWS resources: acronym
('sc-a-203','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.3',$$BAA$$,$$Business Associate Addendum - the HIPAA agreement between AWS and a customer handling protected health information, reviewed and accepted through AWS Artifact rather than assessed by any resource-scanning service.$$),
('sc-a-204','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.3',$$PCI DSS$$,$$Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard - a compliance framework for handling cardholder data, checkable via a Security Hub standard or an AWS Config conformance pack, and pre-mapped in AWS Audit Manager.$$),
('sc-a-205','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.3',$$NIST$$,$$National Institute of Standards and Technology - publisher of NIST 800-53, a security controls catalog available as a Security Hub standard and an Audit Manager framework.$$),
('sc-a-206','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.3',$$SOC 2$$,$$System and Organization Controls report type 2 - an audit report on a service organization's security controls; one of AWS Audit Manager's prebuilt frameworks and a report type available via AWS Artifact.$$),
-- 6.3 evaluate compliance of AWS resources: feynman
('sc-y-103','awsscs','feynman',6,'6.3',$$Explain the difference between AWS Config conformance packs, Security Hub standards, Trusted Advisor, and Audit Manager - when would you reach for each?$$,$$Model answer: All four help you understand compliance, but they answer different questions. Reach for a Config conformance pack when you want a specific, packaged set of Config rules (plus optional auto-remediation) evaluating whether your actual resource configurations match a framework, deployable consistently across accounts - it's rule-level, continuous, and about resource state. Reach for a Security Hub standard when you want a broader, curated set of security best-practice checks (CIS, PCI DSS, NIST 800-53, FSBP) that aggregate findings from Security Hub and partner tools into one dashboard with a security score - it's more about ongoing security posture than one narrow framework's rules. Reach for Trusted Advisor when you want quick, broad best-practice checks across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance that need no framework mapping at all - it's a fast health check, not an audit trail. Reach for Audit Manager when the actual deliverable is an audit: you need continuously collected, organized evidence mapped to a specific framework (SOC 2, PCI DSS, HIPAA) ready to hand to an auditor, not just a pass/fail dashboard. In short: conformance packs check resource configuration, Security Hub checks security posture, Trusted Advisor checks best practices broadly, and Audit Manager builds the evidence file for a human auditor.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 6.1 centralized AWS account management
('sc-q-700','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$An AWS Organizations administrator wants to apply Service Control Policies and tag policies across every member account, but the policies appear to have no effect anywhere. Which prerequisite is most likely missing?$q$,$q$["The organization must be invited by AWS Support first","The organization must be enabled with all features, not consolidated billing only","Every member account must first enable MFA on its root user","AWS Control Tower must be deployed before any policy type works"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SCPs and tag policies are Organizations policy types that only take effect when the organization is enabled with all features; the older consolidated billing only mode supports centralized billing but not policy enforcement. AWS Support enablement, root MFA, and Control Tower are unrelated prerequisites for the policy engine itself.$q$),
('sc-q-701','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$A member account sits in the Prod OU, nested under the Workloads OU, which sits directly under the organization root. A Service Control Policy denying s3:DeleteBucket is attached only to the Workloads OU - the Prod OU and the account itself have no SCP denying this action. Can an administrator in that Prod account, holding an identity policy that grants full S3 access, delete a bucket?$q$,$q$["Yes, because SCPs on a parent OU don't apply to OUs nested beneath it","No, because the account inherits every SCP attached anywhere from the root down to itself, including the Workloads OU's deny","Yes, because SCPs only take effect when attached directly to the account","No, but only because the identity policy itself must also contain an explicit deny"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An account's effective permissions are the intersection of the SCPs attached at every level from the root down through each parent OU to the account itself. The Workloads OU's deny on s3:DeleteBucket therefore applies to every account nested beneath it, including Prod, regardless of how permissive the account's own identity policy is - an explicit SCP deny anywhere in that chain wins.$q$),
('sc-q-702','awsscs',6,'6.1','multi',2,$q$Which of the following are policy types natively supported by AWS Organizations for governing member accounts? (Select THREE)$q$,$q$["Tag policies, standardizing required tag keys and allowed values","Backup policies, enforcing AWS Backup plans across accounts","IAM permission boundaries, capping an individual role's maximum permissions","Resource control policies, capping what resource-based policies can grant"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Tag policies, backup policies, and resource control policies are all policy types you attach to OUs/accounts within AWS Organizations. A permission boundary is an IAM construct attached to a single user or role inside an account - it is not one of the Organizations policy types, even though it also caps maximum permissions.$q$),
('sc-q-703','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',3,$q$A security team wants an OU's accounts to be able to use ONLY an explicitly approved list of services, with everything else blocked by default, rather than allowing everything except a list of banned actions. Which SCP strategy achieves this?$q$,$q$["Deny list strategy, layering Deny statements on top of the default FullAWSAccess SCP","Allow list strategy, replacing the default FullAWSAccess SCP with an explicit Allow naming only approved services","Attaching the default FullAWSAccess SCP with no changes","A permissions boundary applied to every IAM role in the OU"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An allow list strategy removes the default FullAWSAccess SCP and replaces it with an explicit Allow statement naming only approved services and actions, so anything not listed is implicitly denied. A deny list (the default approach) still starts from full access and blocks specific actions - the opposite of "approved only." Permission boundaries apply per identity inside an account, not org-wide.$q$),
('sc-q-704','awsscs',6,'6.1','matching',2,$q$Match each Control Tower guardrail concept to its correct description.$q$,$q${"left":["Preventive guardrail","Detective guardrail","Mandatory guardrail tier","Elective guardrail tier"],"right":["Implemented as an SCP that blocks a disallowed action before it occurs","Implemented as an AWS Config rule that flags a resource already out of compliance","Always enforced by Control Tower and cannot be disabled","Optional guardrail for a specific use case that must be deliberately enabled"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Preventive guardrails use SCPs to stop a disallowed action outright; detective guardrails use Config rules to flag existing noncompliance after the fact. Mandatory guardrails are always on and can't be turned off, while elective guardrails address specific use cases and must be turned on deliberately.$q$),
('sc-q-705','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$A company onboards a new business unit every few weeks and wants each new AWS account it creates to automatically receive the same standardized network configuration, guardrails, and IAM baseline without manual setup. What Control Tower capability provides this?$q$,$q$["AWS Config conformance packs","Control Tower Account Factory","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Backup"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Account Factory automates new-account provisioning with a pre-approved, standardized baseline already applied, eliminating manual per-account configuration and the drift that comes with it. Conformance packs evaluate compliance after the fact, Trusted Advisor checks best practices, and Backup protects data - none of them provision new accounts.$q$),
('sc-q-706','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$A Control Tower-governed account shows a "drifted" status in the Control Tower dashboard after someone manually edited an IAM role that the landing zone originally provisioned. What does this drift status indicate?$q$,$q$["The account's actual configuration has diverged from what Control Tower originally provisioned and governs","The account has exceeded its service quota","The account's SCPs have been automatically updated to a newer version","The account has been suspended for non-payment"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Drift detection flags when a Control Tower-governed resource - an SCP, IAM role, or other guardrail-related configuration - has been changed outside of Control Tower's own management, diverging from the landing zone's intended baseline so the change can be reviewed and reconciled. It has nothing to do with quotas, automatic SCP versioning, or billing suspension.$q$),
('sc-q-707','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',3,$q$A company's SCPs already restrict what its IAM roles can do, but a developer still manages to attach an overly permissive resource-based policy directly to an S3 bucket, granting broader access than intended. Which Organizations policy type is specifically designed to cap this kind of resource-based policy grant, complementing what SCPs already do for identities?$q$,$q$["A tag policy","A Resource Control Policy (RCP)","A backup policy","A permissions boundary"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$RCPs set the maximum permissions that resource-based policies (like an S3 bucket policy or KMS key policy) can grant, closing the gap SCPs leave open since SCPs only cap identity-side permissions. Tag policies standardize tagging, backup policies enforce backup plans, and permission boundaries are an IAM feature scoped to a single identity, not resources.$q$),
('sc-q-708','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',3,$q$A company wants to guarantee that "block public access for new EBS snapshots" stays enabled in every member account forever, with no risk of an administrator disabling it later, without writing and maintaining a custom SCP or Config rule for it. Which Organizations feature fits?$q$,$q$["A declarative policy enforcing that specific account setting org-wide","A tag policy requiring an EBS tag","An AWS Config conformance pack","AWS Trusted Advisor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Declarative policies let you declare a specific account-level setting as the desired state across the org, and AWS enforces and maintains it - no custom SCP or Config rule needed, and it can't drift back to a less secure value. Tag policies only govern tags, conformance packs detect noncompliance rather than prevent it, and Trusted Advisor only advises.$q$),
('sc-q-709','awsscs',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$A security team wants to manage GuardDuty and Security Hub for the whole organization from a dedicated security-tooling account, without granting anyone standing access to the sensitive management account. What should they configure?$q$,$q$["Designate the security-tooling account as the delegated administrator for GuardDuty and Security Hub","Grant the security team AdministratorAccess in the management account","Move the management account's root credentials to the security-tooling account","Create a new organization with the security-tooling account as its root"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Delegating administration of an organization-wide service like GuardDuty or Security Hub to a member account lets that account centrally manage the service for every member without anyone needing standing access to the management account, keeping the most sensitive account's exposure minimal. The other options either misuse the management account or make no operational sense.$q$),
-- 6.2 multi-account security strategy
('sc-q-710','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',1,$q$In a standard AWS landing zone, why is CloudTrail and Config log data delivered to a dedicated Log Archive account rather than staying in each workload account?$q$,$q$["It reduces the AWS bill by consolidating storage costs only","So a compromise in a workload account cannot reach or delete the evidence needed to investigate that very compromise","Because CloudTrail cannot deliver logs within the same account it monitors","So developers can access raw logs more easily for debugging"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Centralizing logs in a locked-down Log Archive account, separate from every workload account, puts the evidence beyond the reach of an attacker or mistaken admin who compromises a workload account - the exact scenario the logs exist to help investigate. CloudTrail can deliver within the same account just fine; cost consolidation and developer convenience are secondary at best, not the primary security rationale.$q$),
('sc-q-711','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$A landing zone designates a dedicated Security Tooling account as the delegated administrator for GuardDuty and Security Hub, and gives security analysts read-only cross-account roles into every workload account from there. What is the main security benefit of this design?$q$,$q$["It centralizes detection tooling and investigative access away from the sensitive management account and any single workload account","It gives analysts write access to remediate findings directly in every account by default","It removes the need for CloudTrail in workload accounts","It allows the Security Tooling account to bypass all SCPs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Running GuardDuty/Security Hub administration and analyst access from a dedicated tooling account keeps that sensitive capability out of the management account (which shouldn't run day-to-day tooling) and out of any single workload account. The design described is read-only for investigation, not a write/remediation grant, doesn't remove the need for logging elsewhere, and SCPs still apply to the tooling account like any other.$q$),
('sc-q-712','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$A company centralizes its Transit Gateway and internet egress in a single dedicated Network account rather than letting each of 40 workload accounts manage its own NAT Gateway and internet gateway. What is the primary blast-radius benefit?$q$,$q$["Workload accounts no longer need their own internet-facing infrastructure, shrinking each one's individual attack surface and letting one team enforce consistent network controls","It eliminates the need for security groups in workload accounts","It guarantees zero network latency between accounts","It allows workload accounts to bypass IAM entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Centralizing shared connectivity means workload accounts don't run their own internet-facing components, reducing what could go wrong (or be attacked) in each one, while the network team applies one consistent set of controls in a single place instead of forty inconsistent ones. It doesn't remove the need for security groups, doesn't guarantee latency characteristics, and has nothing to do with bypassing IAM.$q$),
('sc-q-713','awsscs',6,'6.2','matching',2,$q$Match each landing-zone OU to the type of account it typically contains.$q$,$q${"left":["Security OU","Infrastructure/Shared Services OU","Workloads > Prod OU","Workloads > Sandbox OU"],"right":["Log archive and security-tooling accounts","Centralized networking and shared-tooling accounts","Production workload accounts under the strictest guardrails","Accounts for free experimentation with no path to production data"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The Security OU holds log-archive and tooling accounts; Infrastructure/Shared Services holds centralized networking and shared tools; the Prod OU (nested under Workloads) holds production accounts under the tightest SCPs; and the Sandbox OU isolates experimentation from anything that matters. Grouping accounts this way lets each OU carry guardrails appropriate to its risk level.$q$),
('sc-q-714','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$An account is being decommissioned following an employee offboarding, but the security team wants its data and CloudTrail history preserved for a possible future audit, while ensuring nobody can actively use the account in the meantime. What should they do?$q$,$q$["Move the account to a Suspended OU with a highly restrictive SCP applied","Delete the account immediately to reduce attack surface","Leave the account in its original Workloads OU unchanged","Remove the account from the organization entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A Suspended OU with a near-total-deny SCP keeps the account's resources and log history intact and inaccessible for active use, satisfying both the "preserve for audit" and "prevent misuse" requirements simultaneously. Deleting or removing the account risks destroying needed evidence, and leaving it in its original OU keeps normal access live.$q$),
('sc-q-715','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',3,$q$A newly vended AWS account is placed in a Quarantine OU with a highly restrictive SCP immediately after creation, before being moved into its intended Workloads OU. What risk does this specifically mitigate?$q$,$q$["The account being usable in a state where required logging, guardrails, or tagging haven't yet been confirmed as correctly applied","The account exceeding its EC2 service quota","The account's root user forgetting their password","The account being billed incorrectly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Quarantining a brand-new account until its baseline (CloudTrail, Config, required guardrails, tags) is verified prevents a window where the account exists and is usable but isn't yet actually governed - closing exactly the gap where an unmonitored, unguarded account could become a weak link. Quotas, passwords, and billing aren't what quarantine addresses.$q$),
('sc-q-716','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$A company runs its development, test, and production environments as three separate VPCs inside one shared AWS account, relying on IAM policies and security groups to keep them apart. What blast-radius risk does this design carry that separate accounts would avoid?$q$,$q$["A mistake, overly broad IAM grant, or compromised credential in the dev/test environment shares the same account boundary as production and can potentially reach it","VPCs cannot be created inside the same account","Security groups don't work across VPCs in the same account","IAM policies cannot reference resources in a specific VPC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Account boundaries, not VPC boundaries, are where IAM trust, service quotas, and ultimate blast radius are scoped - so a slip in the dev/test environment shares the same outer boundary as production and, absent extremely careful configuration, can reach it. VPCs and security groups both work fine within a single account, and IAM can reference specific VPCs; the risk is structural, not a technical limitation being described.$q$),
('sc-q-717','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$A company relies entirely on federated SSO through IAM Identity Center for all human access to its AWS accounts. What should they still maintain for the rare case where the identity provider itself is unavailable during an active incident?$q$,$q$["A tightly controlled break-glass IAM identity with strong MFA and heavy audit logging, used only in genuine emergencies","Shared root credentials distributed to the whole security team","No fallback is needed since IAM Identity Center never has outages","A public IAM user with a simple password for quick access"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A break-glass identity provides a narrow, heavily audited emergency path to act when the normal federation path is down, without keeping broad standing access open day to day. Shared root credentials and a simple public IAM user are exactly the kind of standing, poorly audited access break-glass procedures are designed to avoid, and any external dependency like an IdP can have an outage.$q$),
('sc-q-718','awsscs',6,'6.2','ordering',1,$q$Order these landing-zone environments from the least restrictive guardrails to the most restrictive, as a workload matures from prototype to live production traffic.$q$,$q$["Sandbox","Dev","Test","Prod"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Sandbox accounts allow the most freedom since nothing there is production-critical; Dev adds a bit more structure as real feature work begins; Test tightens further to mirror production behavior for validation; Prod carries the strictest guardrails and monitoring because it serves live traffic and real customer data. Guardrails escalate as the blast radius of a mistake grows.$q$),
('sc-q-719','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',1,$q$A company wants engineers to freely try new AWS services and prototype ideas without any risk of accidentally touching customer data or shared production resources. Where should this activity happen?$q$,$q$["Directly in the production account, using a separate IAM role","In a dedicated Sandbox account with no connectivity to production data or shared resources","In the management account, since it has the broadest permissions","In the Log Archive account, since it has spare capacity"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Sandbox account is deliberately isolated from production data and shared resources, so experimentation there - even with mistakes or insecure configuration - cannot affect anything that matters. Using the production or management account for experimentation defeats the purpose of isolation, and the Log Archive account should have no unrelated workloads running in it at all.$q$),
('sc-q-720','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',3,$q$A company must guarantee that all processing and storage of a specific customer dataset stays within the European Union to satisfy a regulatory requirement, alongside its existing US-based workloads. Which multi-account approach BEST supports proving this boundary?$q$,$q$["Isolate the EU workload in its own dedicated account (or OU) restricted to EU regions, separate from the US workload's accounts","Rely on developers to remember to only use EU regions within the shared US account","Tag EU resources and trust the tags for audit purposes","Use a single global account and disable non-EU regions for the whole organization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A dedicated account (or OU) scoped and enforced to EU regions makes the boundary concrete and auditable - SCPs can even enforce the region restriction at the account/OU level. Relying on developer memory or tags alone provides no enforcement, and disabling non-EU regions organization-wide would break the existing US workloads.$q$),
('sc-q-721','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$A company with three largely independent business units gives each one its own OU, with different SCPs, tag policies, and cost-allocation tags per OU. What is the primary security advantage of this structure over one shared OU for all three?$q$,$q$["A security incident or misconfiguration in one business unit's accounts stays contained within that OU's boundary rather than spreading to the others","It eliminates the need for any SCPs entirely","It guarantees each business unit has identical guardrails, simplifying nothing","It removes the need for a management account"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Separate OUs per business unit apply tailored guardrails and contain the blast radius of any one unit's incident or mistake to its own boundary. It doesn't eliminate the need for SCPs, doesn't force identical guardrails (the opposite - it enables tailoring), and every organization still needs exactly one management account.$q$),
('sc-q-722','awsscs',6,'6.2','mcq',3,$q$A security team wants to roll out a new, fairly aggressive SCP across the entire organization but is worried a mistake in the policy could accidentally lock every production account out of a needed action. What deployment approach reduces this risk?$q$,$q$["Attach the SCP to a small Policy Staging OU containing a few non-critical accounts first, then expand to the rest of the organization after validating it","Attach the SCP directly to the organization root immediately for maximum coverage","Skip testing since SCPs cannot cause unintended denials","Attach the SCP only to the management account first"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Testing a new SCP against a small, non-critical set of accounts in a staging OU first limits the blast radius of any mistake in the policy itself, before it's trusted enough to roll out organization-wide. Attaching straight to the root risks breaking everything at once, SCPs absolutely can cause unintended denials (that's the risk being managed), and the management account is not representative of workload account impact and typically shouldn't be an SCP target at all.$q$),
-- 6.3 evaluate compliance of AWS resources
('sc-q-723','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A security team manages 60 AWS accounts and wants a single console view of AWS Config compliance status across all of them and all regions, without logging into each account separately. What should they configure?$q$,$q$["An AWS Config aggregator","A separate Config rule per account","AWS Trusted Advisor","An IAM role with AdministratorAccess in every account"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A Config aggregator consolidates configuration and compliance data from many accounts and regions into one authorized account's view. Deploying rules per account still requires visiting each one, Trusted Advisor checks best practices rather than Config rule compliance, and an admin role in every account is both unnecessary and far too permissive for a read-only visibility need.$q$),
('sc-q-724','awsscs',6,'6.3','matching',2,$q$Match each compliance standard or benchmark to the body or framework it is associated with.$q$,$q${"left":["CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark","PCI DSS","NIST 800-53","AWS Foundational Security Best Practices (FSBP)"],"right":["Published by the Center for Internet Security as a vendor-neutral hardening benchmark","A payment-card-industry framework for protecting cardholder data","A U.S. federal security controls catalog from NIST","An AWS-authored standard reflecting AWS's own security recommendations"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The CIS benchmark comes from the Center for Internet Security, PCI DSS governs payment card data protection, NIST 800-53 is a U.S. federal controls catalog, and FSBP is AWS's own authored best-practice standard - Security Hub can enable and score against all four, but they originate from different bodies.$q$),
('sc-q-725','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A CISO wants a single trackable percentage representing overall security posture across all enabled Security Hub standards, to report progress month over month. What Security Hub feature provides this?$q$,$q$["The Security Hub security score","AWS Trusted Advisor's cost score","A CloudWatch billing alarm","An IAM credential report"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Security Hub security score reflects the proportion of enabled controls currently passing across active standards, giving one number to track posture trends over time. Trusted Advisor doesn't score cost this way, a billing alarm tracks spend, and a credential report only covers IAM credential hygiene.$q$),
('sc-q-726','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',2,$q$A company runs Security Hub in 30 accounts across 4 regions and wants to enable the same standards and controls everywhere from one place, rather than configuring each account/region combination individually. What should they use?$q$,$q$["Security Hub central configuration from a designated administrator account","A separate Security Hub subscription per account with no linkage","AWS Config conformance packs instead of Security Hub","Manually toggling standards in all 120 account/region combinations"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Central configuration lets a designated Security Hub administrator account define and push standards/controls across every linked account and region at once. Separate unlinked subscriptions and manual per-combination toggling are exactly the operational burden central configuration removes, and conformance packs are an AWS Config feature, not a replacement for Security Hub's own configuration.$q$),
('sc-q-727','awsscs',6,'6.3','multi',2,$q$A team wants AWS Config to not just detect a noncompliant resource (like an unencrypted EBS volume) but automatically fix it. Which statements about achieving this are TRUE? (Select TWO)$q$,$q$["A remediation action, typically an SSM Automation document, must be attached to the Config rule","Config rules can fix resources on their own with no additional configuration","The remediation can be configured to run automatically or require manual approval first","Automatic remediation is only possible for Security Hub findings, never for Config rule noncompliance"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Config itself only evaluates and reports compliance; auto-remediation requires attaching a remediation action, usually an SSM Automation document, to the rule, and that remediation can be set to fire automatically or wait for manual approval. Config rules cannot self-remediate without that attached action, and remediation is a Config capability in its own right, not something limited to Security Hub findings.$q$),
('sc-q-728','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A security team wants to see Trusted Advisor check results for all 40 accounts in their organization from a single place, rather than each account owner checking their own account individually. What should they enable?$q$,$q$["Trusted Advisor's organizational view, consolidating checks across all member accounts","A separate Business Support plan purchased individually in each account with no aggregation","AWS Config conformance packs","Security Hub's FSBP standard"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Trusted Advisor's organizational view aggregates check results from every member account into the management (or delegated administrator) account's dashboard. Buying support per account without aggregation still leaves the team checking each one separately, and conformance packs / the FSBP standard are different services entirely, not Trusted Advisor aggregation.$q$),
('sc-q-729','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$A company on the Basic support plan notices it only sees a small number of core Trusted Advisor checks (like S3 bucket permissions and service limits), while a partner company on Business Support sees the full best-practice check catalog across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance. What explains the difference?$q$,$q$["Trusted Advisor's full set of best-practice checks requires a Business or Enterprise support plan; Basic/Developer plans only include a limited core set","Trusted Advisor checks are randomly assigned per account regardless of support plan","The Basic plan account must enable checks manually one at a time, which the company forgot to do","Trusted Advisor requires AWS Organizations to unlock additional checks, independent of support plan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The full Trusted Advisor best-practice check catalog is a benefit of Business or Enterprise Support; Basic and Developer plans get only a small set of core checks (like a handful of security and service-limit checks) at no extra cost. Checks aren't randomly assigned, there's no per-check manual unlock step, and Organizations membership by itself doesn't change the support-plan-gated check catalog.$q$),
('sc-q-730','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A compliance team needs to start collecting evidence for an upcoming PCI DSS assessment quickly, without spending weeks manually mapping every PCI control to a specific evidence source. What AWS Audit Manager feature helps most directly?$q$,$q$["A prebuilt PCI DSS framework with controls already mapped to evidence sources","A blank custom framework the team must fully define themselves","AWS Artifact's PCI attestation of compliance report","AWS Trusted Advisor's cost checks"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Audit Manager ships prebuilt frameworks (including PCI DSS) with controls already mapped to the AWS evidence sources that satisfy them, letting evidence collection start immediately. A blank custom framework requires exactly the manual mapping work being avoided, AWS Artifact only provides AWS's own PCI attestation (not evidence about the customer's workloads), and Trusted Advisor's cost checks are unrelated to PCI evidence.$q$),
('sc-q-731','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',2,$q$A company wants to deploy the same AWS Config conformance pack, with the same rules and remediation actions, consistently across every account in a specific OU, and see one aggregated compliance status for the whole deployment. What should they use?$q$,$q$["An organization conformance pack deployed across the OU, paired with a Config aggregator for the combined view","A separate, independently maintained conformance pack manually created in each account","A single Security Hub standard enabled in only the management account","AWS Backup policies applied to the OU"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An organization conformance pack deploys identical rules/remediations to every account in a target OU from one definition, and pairing it with a Config aggregator gives the single consolidated compliance view. Manually recreating the pack per account risks drift between copies, a Security Hub standard in only the management account doesn't evaluate the other accounts at all, and Backup policies govern backups, not Config rule compliance.$q$),
('sc-q-732','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',2,$q$A customer asks their AWS account team for AWS's own SOC 2 report to satisfy their vendor security questionnaire. Which AWS resource should they be directed to, as distinct from a service that would instead assess the CUSTOMER's own workload compliance?$q$,$q$["AWS Artifact, for AWS's own compliance reports and agreements","AWS Audit Manager, which assesses the customer's own AWS resources against a framework","AWS Config conformance packs, which evaluate the customer's resource configurations","AWS Trusted Advisor, which checks the customer's account against best practices"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AWS Artifact is the self-service portal for downloading AWS's own audit reports and agreements (SOC reports, PCI attestation, a BAA) - exactly what's needed to show AWS's compliance in a vendor questionnaire. Audit Manager, conformance packs, and Trusted Advisor all assess the customer's own resources or account, not AWS's compliance posture.$q$),
('sc-q-733','awsscs',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$A team needs to enforce and continuously check ten highly specific, custom-written rules unique to their internal security policy, not part of any public framework, deployable consistently to every account in an OU. Which approach fits better than simply enabling a built-in Security Hub standard?$q$,$q$["An AWS Config conformance pack built from their custom rules, deployed org-wide","Enabling the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark standard in Security Hub, since it covers all custom needs","Enabling AWS FSBP only, since it is fully customizable per account","Waiting for AWS to add the company's specific rules to a future Security Hub standard"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A conformance pack is a flexible package of Config rules - including entirely custom ones - deployable consistently across accounts, which fits a bespoke internal policy that no public framework covers. Security Hub's built-in standards (CIS, FSBP, etc.) are fixed, curated control sets you can't rewrite with arbitrary custom logic, and there's no mechanism to wait for AWS to add company-specific rules to them.$q$);
